## Worked Example: A Small Price Calculator

This example combines arithmetic, casting, autoboxing, and safe comparison
into one realistic snippet — a shopping cart discount calculator.

```java
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Objects;

public class PriceCalculator {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Integer> pricesInCents = List.of(1999, 500, 250); // autoboxed Integers

        int totalCents = 0;
        for (int price : pricesInCents) { // unboxing happens here automatically
            totalCents += price;
        }

        double totalDollars = totalCents / 100.0; // widen to double before dividing
        double discountRate = 0.10;
        double discounted = totalDollars * (1 - discountRate);

        System.out.printf("Subtotal: $%.2f%n", totalDollars);
        System.out.printf("After 10%% discount: $%.2f%n", discounted);

        String couponCode = null;
        String appliedCode = "NONE";
        System.out.println("Coupon equal to NONE? " + Objects.equals(couponCode, "NONE"));
        System.out.println("Applied code: " + appliedCode);

        // Overflow-safe total using Math.addExact
        try {
            int huge = Integer.MAX_VALUE;
            int overflowed = Math.addExact(huge, totalCents);
            System.out.println(overflowed);
        } catch (ArithmeticException e) {
            System.out.println("Total would overflow int range!");
        }
    }
}
```

Notice how `pricesInCents` autoboxes `int` literals into `Integer` objects
to be stored in the `List`, then unboxes them back to `int` inside the
for-loop; the division uses `100.0` to force a `double` result instead of
truncating; and `Math.addExact` guards against silent overflow.

## Recap

- Integer division truncates toward zero; involve a `double`/`float`
  operand to get a fractional result.
- Widening conversions happen automatically; narrowing requires an explicit
  cast and can silently lose data or wrap around on overflow.
- `&&`/`||` short-circuit; `&`/`|` on booleans do not — prefer the
  short-circuiting forms unless you have a specific reason not to.
- Autoboxing/unboxing bridge primitives and their wrapper classes
  automatically, but risk `NullPointerException` on unboxing `null` and
  subtle `==` bugs due to the Integer cache.
- Always use `.equals()` (or `Objects.equals` for null-safety) to compare
  the *content* of reference types; `==` compares identity.

codeExamples.code:
int a = 7, b = 2;
System.out.println(a / b);          // 3 (integer division)
System.out.println((double) a / b); // 3.5

double pi = 3.99;
int truncated = (int) pi;           // 3, explicit narrowing cast

import java.util.List;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Integer a = 100, b = 100;
        System.out.println(a == b);        // true (Integer cache)

        Integer c = 200, d = 200;
        System.out.println(c == d);        // false (outside cache)
        System.out.println(c.equals(d));   // true (always safe)
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(Integer.MAX_VALUE + 1); // silently wraps to negative
        try {
            Math.addExact(Integer.MAX_VALUE, 1);
        } catch (ArithmeticException e) {
            System.out.println("overflow detected: " + e.getMessage());
        }
    }
}

