## Writing to the Console

Java's most basic output tools are `System.out.println`, `System.out.print`,
and `System.out.printf`, all methods on the `PrintStream` object
`System.out`.

```java
System.out.print("No newline here");
System.out.println(" — and now there is one");
System.out.println(42);          // works with any type via overloads
```

`printf` (and its cousin `String.format`) supports C-style format
specifiers for precise formatting:

```java
System.out.printf("%s scored %d points (%.2f%%)%n", "Ada", 95, 95.456);
// Ada scored 95 points (95.46%)
```

Common specifiers: `%d` (integer), `%s` (any object via `toString()`),
`%f`/`%.Nf` (floating point with N decimal places), `%c` (char), `%b`
(boolean), and `%n` for a platform-independent newline (prefer it over
`\n` in `printf`/`format`).

## Reading Input With Scanner

`java.util.Scanner` wraps an input source — typically `System.in` for
console input — and provides typed methods for reading tokens.

```java
import java.util.Scanner;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);
        System.out.print("Enter your name: ");
        String name = sc.nextLine();

        System.out.print("Enter your age: ");
        int age = sc.nextInt();

        System.out.printf("%s will turn %d next year%n", name, age + 1);
    }
}
```

`Scanner` offers `nextLine()` (a full line of text), `nextInt()`,
`nextDouble()`, `nextBoolean()`, and `hasNext()`/`hasNextLine()` to check
whether more input is available before reading (useful for loops that
consume all of stdin).

## Standard Streams: `out`, `err`, and `in`

Java exposes three predefined streams on the `System` class:

- **`System.out`** — standard output.
- **`System.err`** — standard error, typically unbuffered and shown
  immediately, used for diagnostics/error messages.
- **`System.in`** — standard input, a raw `InputStream` that `Scanner`
  (or `BufferedReader`) wraps to make convenient.

```java
System.out.println("normal output");
System.err.println("something went wrong");
```

## The `nextInt()` / `nextLine()` Trap

The single most common `Scanner` bug: mixing `nextInt()`/`nextDouble()` with
`nextLine()`. Token-based methods like `nextInt()` consume only the number
itself, **leaving the trailing newline character in the buffer**. The very
next `nextLine()` call then reads that leftover empty remainder instead of
waiting for new input.

```java
Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);
System.out.print("Age: ");
int age = sc.nextInt();       // reads "25", leaves "\n" in the buffer

System.out.print("Name: ");
String name = sc.nextLine();  // BUG: immediately reads the leftover "" and returns empty!
```

The idiomatic fix is to consume the leftover newline explicitly:

```java
int age = sc.nextInt();
sc.nextLine();                 // consume the leftover newline
String name = sc.nextLine();   // now reads the actual next line correctly
```

Many experienced developers sidestep this entirely by reading everything as
lines and parsing manually:

```java
int age = Integer.parseInt(sc.nextLine().trim());
String name = sc.nextLine();
```

## `BufferedReader` for High-Volume Input

`Scanner` is convenient but relatively slow because of its regex-based
tokenizing. For performance-sensitive input (competitive programming,
reading huge files), `BufferedReader` combined with `StreamTokenizer` or
manual parsing is faster:

```java
import java.io.BufferedReader;
import java.io.InputStreamReader;
import java.io.IOException;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws IOException {
        BufferedReader br = new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(System.in));
        String line = br.readLine();
        int n = Integer.parseInt(line.trim());
        System.out.println(n * n);
    }
}
```

`BufferedReader.readLine()` throws a checked `IOException`, which must be
declared (`throws IOException`) or caught.

## `printf` Format Specifier Details

Format specifiers can include width and flags, e.g. `%5d` right-pads to a
minimum width of 5 characters, `%-5d` left-aligns, and `%05d` zero-pads:

```java
System.out.printf("[%5d]%n", 42);   // [   42]
System.out.printf("[%-5d]%n", 42);  // [42   ]
System.out.printf("[%05d]%n", 42);  // [00042]
```

Passing the wrong argument type to a specifier (e.g., a `String` to `%d`)
throws a runtime `IllegalFormatConversionException` — the compiler cannot
catch format-string mismatches.

## Idiom: One `Scanner` Instance, Reused

Create a single `Scanner` wrapping `System.in` once, near the top of
`main`, and reuse it for the whole program rather than creating multiple
instances — multiple `Scanner`s over the same stream can lose buffered
input in confusing ways.

```java
Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);
// use sc for every subsequent read in this program
```

## Idiom: Validate Input in a Loop

A robust pattern for reading a valid integer, re-prompting on bad input:

```java
Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);
int value = -1;
while (true) {
    System.out.print("Enter a positive number: ");
    if (sc.hasNextInt()) {
        value = sc.nextInt();
        if (value > 0) break;
    } else {
        sc.next(); // discard the invalid token so we don't loop forever
    }
    System.out.println("Invalid input, try again.");
}
```

## Common Pitfall: Not Closing/Managing Streams

`Scanner` implements `AutoCloseable`. For `System.in`-backed scanners this
matters less (closing it closes stdin for the whole JVM, so most simple
console programs simply never close it), but for a `Scanner` wrapping a
file, always close it — ideally with try-with-resources (covered in a later
module):

```java
try (Scanner fileScanner = new Scanner(new java.io.File("data.txt"))) {
    while (fileScanner.hasNextLine()) {
        System.out.println(fileScanner.nextLine());
    }
} catch (java.io.FileNotFoundException e) {
    System.err.println("File not found: " + e.getMessage());
}
```

## Common Pitfall: Locale-Sensitive Parsing

`Scanner.nextDouble()` and `nextFloat()` are **locale-sensitive** — in
locales that use a comma as the decimal separator, `nextDouble()` on `"3.14"`
can throw `InputMismatchException`. Production code that must be
locale-independent often forces a locale explicitly:

```java
Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);
sc.useLocale(java.util.Locale.US);
```

## Common Pitfall: `printf` Argument/Specifier Mismatch

```java
System.out.printf("%d", "not a number"); // throws IllegalFormatConversionException at runtime
```

Because format strings aren't type-checked at compile time, always
double-check that specifier and argument types line up — a classic source
of production runtime errors.

## Worked Example: A Simple Interactive Quiz

This example combines `Scanner`, the `nextInt`/`nextLine` pitfall fix, input
validation, and `printf` formatting.

```java
import java.util.Scanner;

public class QuizApp {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);

        System.out.print("What's your name? ");
        String name = sc.nextLine();

        System.out.print("How many years have you coded? ");
        int years;
        while (true) {
            if (sc.hasNextInt()) {
                years = sc.nextInt();
                sc.nextLine(); // consume leftover newline after nextInt()
                break;
            } else {
                System.out.print("Please enter a whole number: ");
                sc.next(); // discard the bad token
            }
        }

        double score = years >= 5 ? 95.5 : years >= 1 ? 70.0 : 40.0;

        System.out.printf("%n--- Results ---%n");
        System.out.printf("%-10s %s%n", "Name:", name);
        System.out.printf("%-10s %d years%n", "Experience:", years);
        System.out.printf("%-10s %.1f%%%n", "Score:", score);

        if (score < 60) {
            System.err.println("Warning: score below passing threshold.");
        }
    }
}
```

Notice how the loop guards against non-integer input with `hasNextInt()`,
explicitly consumes the leftover newline with `sc.nextLine()` right after
`nextInt()`, and uses `printf`'s width/alignment flags (`%-10s`) to build a
neatly formatted report — with `System.err` reserved for the warning line.

## Recap

- `System.out.println/print/printf` write formatted output; `%d %s %f %n`
  are the specifiers you'll use constantly.
- `Scanner(System.in)` is the standard way to read console input;
  `BufferedReader` is a faster low-level alternative for heavy input.
- The classic `nextInt()` followed by `nextLine()` bug leaves a stray
  newline in the buffer — consume it explicitly or read everything as lines.
- `hasNextInt()`/`hasNextLine()` let you validate input before consuming it,
  enabling robust re-prompt loops.
- `System.err` is a separate stream from `System.out`, conventionally used
  for diagnostics and error messages.

codeExamples.code:
import java.util.Scanner;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);
        String name = sc.nextLine();
        int age = Integer.parseInt(sc.nextLine());
        System.out.printf("%s will turn %d next year%n", name, age + 1);
    }
}

import java.util.Scanner;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);
        int age = sc.nextInt();
        sc.nextLine(); // consume leftover newline
        String name = sc.nextLine();
        System.out.println(name + " is " + age);
    }
}

import java.util.Scanner;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);
        int value = -1;
        while (true) {
            if (sc.hasNextInt()) {
                value = sc.nextInt();
                if (value > 0) break;
            } else {
                sc.next();
            }
        }
        System.out.println("Got: " + value);
    }
}

