## Branching With `if`/`else`

Java's `if`/`else if`/`else` branches on boolean expressions. Crucially,
unlike C, the condition **must** be an actual `boolean` — there's no
implicit conversion from `int` to `boolean`.

```java
int score = 82;
if (score >= 90) {
    System.out.println("A");
} else if (score >= 80) {
    System.out.println("B");
} else {
    System.out.println("C or below");
}
```

```java
int x = 1;
// if (x) { }     // compile error — int is not a boolean
if (x != 0) { }    // correct — an explicit boolean expression
```

## The Three Classic Loops

```java
// for: known number of iterations
for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
    System.out.println(i);
}

// while: condition checked before each iteration
int n = 5;
while (n > 0) {
    System.out.println(n);
    n--;
}

// do-while: body runs at least once, condition checked after
int attempts = 0;
do {
    attempts++;
} while (attempts < 3);
```

## The Enhanced For-Loop

For iterating over arrays or any `Iterable` (like collections), the
enhanced `for (Type item : collection)` form is clearer and less
error-prone than manual indexing:

```java
int[] nums = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};
for (int n : nums) {
    if (n % 2 == 0) continue;
    System.out.println(n);
}
```

## Classic `switch` and Modern Switch Expressions

The classic `switch` statement falls through by default unless each case
ends in `break`:

```java
int day = 3;
switch (day) {
    case 1:
        System.out.println("Mon");
        break;
    case 2:
        System.out.println("Tue");
        break;
    default:
        System.out.println("Other");
}
```

Since Java 14, **switch expressions** using `->` return a value directly
and don't fall through — the preferred modern style:

```java
int day = 3;
String name = switch (day) {
    case 1 -> "Mon";
    case 2 -> "Tue";
    case 3 -> "Wed";
    default -> "Unknown";
};
System.out.println(name);
```

## `break`, `continue`, and Labeled Loops

`break` exits the nearest enclosing loop or switch; `continue` skips to the
next iteration. Java also supports **labeled** break/continue to control an
*outer* loop from inside a nested one — something many languages lack:

```java
outer:
for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
    for (int j = 0; j < 3; j++) {
        if (j == 1) continue outer;  // skip to next i, not just next j
        if (i == 2) break outer;      // exit both loops entirely
        System.out.println(i + "," + j);
    }
}
```

Without the label, `break`/`continue` only ever affect the innermost loop.

## Switch Expressions With `yield` and Pattern Matching

A switch expression case can contain a full block, using `yield` to produce
the value:

```java
int score = 75;
String grade = switch (score / 10) {
    case 10, 9 -> "A";
    case 8 -> "B";
    case 7 -> {
        System.out.println("Borderline C/B");
        yield "C";
    }
    default -> "F";
};
```

Notice `case 10, 9 ->` — multiple labels can share one arm, comma-separated.

Modern Java (17+) also supports **pattern matching for `switch`**
(finalized in Java 21, previewable earlier), letting a switch branch on an
object's runtime type directly:

```java
Object value = 42;
String description = switch (value) {
    case Integer i when i > 0 -> "positive int: " + i;
    case Integer i -> "non-positive int: " + i;
    case String s -> "string of length " + s.length();
    default -> "something else";
};
```

## Fall-Through Is a Deliberate Feature (and a Trap)

Classic `switch` fall-through is occasionally used deliberately to group
cases with shared behavior — but forgetting a `break` is one of the
longest-running sources of Java bugs:

```java
int month = 4;
switch (month) {
    case 12: case 1: case 2:
        System.out.println("Winter");
        break;
    case 3: case 4: case 5:
        System.out.println("Spring");
        break;
    // forgetting break here would silently fall into the next case
    default:
        System.out.println("Other season");
}
```

Switch *expressions* (`->`) eliminate this entire class of bug since each
arm is isolated by design.

## Infinite Loops and `while (true)`

`while (true) { ... break; ... }` is an idiomatic way to express "loop until
some internal condition is met," especially when the exit condition is more
naturally checked in the middle of the loop body rather than at the top.

## Idiom: Prefer Switch Expressions Over Classic Switch

Modern Java style strongly favors switch *expressions* over switch
*statements* whenever you're producing a value — they're more concise, can't
fall through by accident, and the compiler checks exhaustiveness for enums
and sealed types.

```java
enum Direction { NORTH, SOUTH, EAST, WEST }

Direction d = Direction.NORTH;
int angle = switch (d) {
    case NORTH -> 0;
    case EAST -> 90;
    case SOUTH -> 180;
    case WEST -> 270;
}; // no default needed — compiler verifies all enum values are covered
```

## Idiom: Guard Clauses Instead of Deep Nesting

Experienced Java developers favor early returns ("guard clauses") over
deeply nested `if`/`else`, which quickly becomes hard to read:

```java
// Avoid:
static String classify(int age) {
    if (age >= 0) {
        if (age < 13) {
            return "child";
        } else {
            if (age < 20) return "teen";
            else return "adult";
        }
    } else {
        return "invalid";
    }
}

// Prefer:
static String classifyClean(int age) {
    if (age < 0) return "invalid";
    if (age < 13) return "child";
    if (age < 20) return "teen";
    return "adult";
}
```

## Common Pitfall: Assignment vs. Equality in Conditions

```java
boolean flag = false;
// if (flag = true) { }   // compiles! assigns true to flag, condition always true — a classic bug
if (flag == true) { }      // correct, though `if (flag)` alone is more idiomatic
```

Since Java requires a genuine `boolean` in conditions, this exact bug is
*less* dangerous than in C (where `if (x = 5)` silently compiles using
non-boolean ints), but it can still slip through when both sides are
already `boolean`.

## Common Pitfall: Off-by-One in Loop Bounds

```java
int[] arr = {10, 20, 30};
for (int i = 0; i <= arr.length; i++) {   // BUG: should be i < arr.length
    System.out.println(arr[i]);            // throws ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException on the last iteration
}
```

Always double-check `<` vs. `<=` against `array.length`, and prefer the
enhanced for-loop whenever the index itself isn't needed — it eliminates
this class of bug entirely.

## Common Pitfall: Forgetting `break` in Classic Switch

Covered above, but worth repeating as a pitfall in its own right: a missing
`break` in a classic `switch` statement causes silent fall-through into
the next case's code, executing logic that wasn't intended to run.

## Worked Example: A Simple Grading Report

This combines `if`/`else`, the enhanced for-loop, labeled loops, and a
switch expression into one small program that grades a list of scores.

```java
import java.util.List;

public class GradingReport {
    static String letterGrade(int score) {
        return switch (score / 10) {
            case 10, 9 -> "A";
            case 8 -> "B";
            case 7 -> "C";
            case 6 -> "D";
            default -> "F";
        };
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Integer> scores = List.of(95, 82, 71, 40, 88, -5);

        int passingCount = 0;
        int index = 0;

        scan:
        for (int score : scores) {
            index++;

            if (score < 0) {
                System.out.println("Invalid score at position " + index + ", stopping scan.");
                break scan; // labeled break, though only one loop here for clarity
            }

            String grade = letterGrade(score);
            System.out.println("Score " + score + " -> " + grade);

            if (grade.equals("F")) {
                continue; // don't count failing grades as passing
            }
            passingCount++;
        }

        System.out.println("Passing count: " + passingCount);
    }
}
```

Walking through it: the enhanced for-loop iterates scores without manual
indexing; `letterGrade` uses a switch *expression* to map a numeric score to
a letter grade cleanly; a labeled `break scan` demonstrates how to exit early
from a specific loop when invalid data appears; and `continue` skips
counting failing grades without needing nested `if`/`else`.

## Recap

- `if` conditions must be genuine booleans — no implicit int-to-bool
  conversion in Java.
- `for`, `while`, `do-while`, and the enhanced `for (Type x : collection)`
  cover every common iteration need.
- Switch *expressions* (`case X ->`) return a value, can't fall through by
  accident, and let the compiler check exhaustiveness for enums.
- Labeled `break`/`continue` let you control an outer loop from inside
  nested loops.
- Classic switch fall-through and off-by-one loop bounds are the two most
  common control-flow bugs — guard clauses and enhanced for-loops sidestep
  both.

