## Anatomy of a Method

A method declaration has a return type (or `void`), a name, a parameter
list, and a body. Methods belong to a class — `static` methods belong to
the class itself, instance methods belong to individual objects.

```java
public class MathUtils {
    static int add(int a, int b) {
        return a + b;
    }

    static int square(int n) {
        return n * n;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(add(2, 3));      // 5
        System.out.println(square(add(1, 2))); // 9
    }
}
```

- The return type must match what `return` produces (or be `void`, meaning
  no `return value;` is allowed — only a bare `return;` to exit early).
- Parameters are typed just like variables.

## Method Overloading

**Overloading** lets multiple methods share a name as long as their
parameter lists differ (in count or type). The compiler picks the right one
at compile time based on the argument types.

```java
public class Main {
    static int add(int a, int b) { return a + b; }
    static double add(double a, double b) { return a + b; }
    static String add(String a, String b) { return a + b; }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(add(2, 3));         // 5 (int version)
        System.out.println(add(2.5, 3.5));     // 6.0 (double version)
        System.out.println(add("foo", "bar")); // foobar (String version)
    }
}
```

## Pass-by-Value, Always

Java is **always pass-by-value** — there is no pass-by-reference. For
primitives, the value itself is copied. For objects, the value copied is
the **reference** (the pointer), not the object — so mutating the object's
fields through that reference is visible to the caller, but reassigning the
parameter to point elsewhere is not.

```java
static void increment(int x) {
    x = x + 1;              // only changes the local copy
}

static void addItem(java.util.List<String> list) {
    list.add("new item");   // mutates the shared object — visible to the caller
}

static void reassign(java.util.List<String> list) {
    list = new java.util.ArrayList<>(); // only rebinds the local variable, caller unaffected
}
```

## Variable Scope

A variable's scope is the block `{}` it's declared in. Parameters are
scoped to the whole method body; a variable declared inside a `for` loop or
`if` block only exists within that block.

## Idiom: Keep Methods Short and Single-Purpose

A method should ideally do one clearly-named thing. If you find yourself
writing a comment like `// now calculate the discount`, that's usually a
sign the logic deserves its own method:

```java
static double calculateDiscount(double price, boolean isMember) {
    return isMember ? price * 0.9 : price;
}

static double finalPrice(double price, boolean isMember, double taxRate) {
    double discounted = calculateDiscount(price, isMember);
    return discounted * (1 + taxRate);
}
```

## Idiom: Overload for Convenience, Delegate to One Canonical Method

A common, clean pattern is to have several overloads all funnel into one
"canonical" implementation with sensible defaults for the rest:

```java
static String greet(String name, String greeting) {
    return greeting + ", " + name + "!";
}

static String greet(String name) {
    return greet(name, "Hello"); // delegates, avoiding duplicated logic
}
```

## Common Pitfall: Believing Java Passes Objects "By Reference"

This is the single most common Java misconception. Java passes the
*reference value* by copy — it is **not** pass-by-reference in the C++/C#
`ref` sense:

```java
static void reassignExample(StringBuilder sb) {
    sb = new StringBuilder("replaced"); // only rebinds the local parameter
}

public static void main(String[] args) {
    StringBuilder original = new StringBuilder("original");
    reassignExample(original);
    System.out.println(original); // still prints "original" — caller's reference unaffected
}
```

If `reassignExample` had instead called `sb.append("!")`, the caller's
`StringBuilder` *would* show the change — because that mutates the shared
object rather than rebinding the local variable.

## Common Pitfall: Shadowing Fields With Parameters

A parameter named the same as a field silently shadows it inside the
method — `this.` is required to refer to the field:

```java
class Point {
    int x, y;
    Point(int x, int y) {
        this.x = x;   // this.x is the field, x is the parameter
        this.y = y;
        // x = x;      // would be a no-op — parameter assigned to itself!
    }
}
```

## Common Pitfall: Unbounded Recursion Without a Base Case

Every recursive method needs a base case that stops the recursion — a
missing or unreachable base case leads directly to `StackOverflowError`,
often only manifesting on large inputs that weren't tested.

## Worked Example: A Small Utility Class

This example combines overloading, varargs, recursion, and pass-by-value
semantics into a small `Stats` utility.

```java
public class Stats {
    // Canonical implementation using varargs
    static double average(double... values) {
        if (values.length == 0) return 0.0;
        double sum = 0;
        for (double v : values) {
            sum += v;
        }
        return sum / values.length;
    }

    // Overload for convenience over an int array
    static double average(int[] values) {
        double[] asDoubles = new double[values.length];
        for (int i = 0; i < values.length; i++) {
            asDoubles[i] = values[i]; // widening int -> double happens automatically
        }
        return average(asDoubles);
    }

    // Recursive helper — sum of digits
    static int digitSum(int n) {
        if (n < 10) return n; // base case
        return n % 10 + digitSum(n / 10);
    }

    // Demonstrates pass-by-value with a mutable array
    static void doubleAllValues(int[] arr) {
        for (int i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
            arr[i] *= 2; // mutates the shared array — visible to the caller
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(average(1.5, 2.5, 3.0));      // varargs version
        System.out.println(average(new int[]{4, 5, 6})); // overload version
        System.out.println(digitSum(9875));               // recursive: 9+8+7+5 = 29

        int[] scores = {10, 20, 30};
        doubleAllValues(scores);
        System.out.println(java.util.Arrays.toString(scores)); // [20, 40, 60]
    }
}
```

Notice `average(int[])` delegates to `average(double... values)` rather
than duplicating the summing logic; `digitSum` recurses with a clear base
case (`n < 10`); and `doubleAllValues` demonstrates that mutating array
contents through a passed reference is visible to the caller, even though
Java is strictly pass-by-value.

## Recap

- Every method has an explicit return type or void, and belongs to a class
  (static methods to the class, instance methods to objects).
- Java is always pass-by-value; for objects, that value is a copy of the
  reference, so field mutations are visible but reassignment isn't.
- Overloading means same name, different parameter lists, resolved at
  compile time using a most-specific-match rule.
- Varargs (`Type... name`) let a method accept a variable number of
  arguments, and must be the last parameter.
- Each method call gets its own stack frame; unbounded recursion without a
  base case causes StackOverflowError.
- Local variables' scope is limited to the block they're declared in;
  parameter names can shadow fields, requiring `this.` to disambiguate.

