## Worked Example: Word Frequency Counter

This combines arrays, `Map`, `StringBuilder`, streams, and lambdas into a
small but realistic word-frequency counter.

```java
import java.util.*;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

public class WordFrequency {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String text = "the quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog the fox runs";
        String[] words = text.split(" "); // fixed-size array of tokens

        Map<String, Integer> frequency = new HashMap<>();
        for (String word : words) {
            frequency.merge(word, 1, Integer::sum); // idiomatic increment-or-insert
        }

        // Stream pipeline: sort entries by frequency descending, take top 3
        List<Map.Entry<String, Integer>> top3 = frequency.entrySet().stream()
                .sorted((a, b) -> b.getValue() - a.getValue())
                .limit(3)
                .collect(Collectors.toList());

        StringBuilder report = new StringBuilder("Top words:\n");
        for (Map.Entry<String, Integer> entry : top3) {
            report.append(entry.getKey())
                  .append(" -> ")
                  .append(entry.getValue())
                  .append("\n");
        }
        System.out.print(report);

        long uniqueWordCount = Arrays.stream(words).distinct().count();
        System.out.println("Unique words: " + uniqueWordCount);
    }
}
```

`frequency.merge(word, 1, Integer::sum)` is the idiomatic one-liner for
"increment the count if present, otherwise insert 1" — far cleaner than a
manual `containsKey` check. The stream pipeline sorts map entries by value
and takes the top 3, and `Arrays.stream(words).distinct().count()`
demonstrates turning a plain array into a stream for a quick unique-count.

## Recap

- Arrays are fixed-size; `ArrayList`, `HashSet`, and `HashMap` (via the
  Collections Framework) grow dynamically and use generics for type safety.
- `String` is immutable — every "modifying" method returns a new String;
  use `StringBuilder` for efficient repeated concatenation.
- `HashMap`/`HashSet` depend on correct, consistent `hashCode()`/`equals()`
  overrides for custom key/element types.
- The Stream API (`map`/`filter`/`sorted`/`collect`) plus lambdas and method
  references (`String::toUpperCase`) enable concise, functional-style data
  transformations; streams are lazy and single-use.
- Never mutate a collection directly while iterating it with a for-each
  loop — use `removeIf` or an explicit `Iterator`.
- `==` compares identity for arrays (use `Arrays.equals`), while `List`/
  `Set`/`Map` correctly override `.equals()` for content comparison.

