## Classes, Fields, Methods, and Constructors

A class bundles state (fields) and behavior (methods). A **constructor**
initializes a new instance's fields when created with `new`.

```java
public class Dog {
    private String name;   // state
    private int age;

    Dog(String name, int age) {  // constructor
        this.name = name;
        this.age = age;
    }

    String bark() {               // behavior
        return name + " says Woof!";
    }
}

Dog rex = new Dog("Rex", 3);
System.out.println(rex.bark());
```

## Encapsulation With Access Modifiers

**Encapsulation** hides internal state behind a controlled public API.
`private` fields are typically exposed only through getters/setters, so the
class can validate or change its internal representation later without
breaking callers.

```java
public class BankAccount {
    private double balance;   // hidden from outside code

    public double getBalance() { return balance; }

    public void deposit(double amount) {
        if (amount <= 0) throw new IllegalArgumentException("must be positive");
        balance += amount;
    }
}
```

Modifiers, from most to least restrictive: `private` (class only),
package-private/default (same package), `protected` (same package +
subclasses), `public` (everywhere).

## Inheritance and Polymorphism

`extends` establishes an inheritance relationship; `@Override` marks a
subclass method that replaces a parent's implementation. Calling an
overridden method through a parent-typed reference still dispatches to the
subclass's version — this is **polymorphism**.

```java
abstract class Animal {
    protected String name;
    Animal(String name) { this.name = name; }
    abstract String speak();     // no body — subclasses must implement it
}

class Dog2 extends Animal {
    Dog2(String name) { super(name); }  // calls the parent constructor
    @Override
    String speak() { return name + " says Woof!"; }
}

Animal a = new Dog2("Rex");  // reference type Animal, actual object Dog2
System.out.println(a.speak()); // "Rex says Woof!" — dispatches to Dog2's override
```

## Interfaces: Pure Contracts

An `interface` declares method signatures (a contract) with no state; a
class fulfills it via `implements`. A class can implement multiple
interfaces, unlike single class inheritance.

```java
interface Movable {
    void move(int dx, int dy);
}

class Robot implements Movable {
    int x, y;
    @Override
    public void move(int dx, int dy) { x += dx; y += dy; }
}
```

## Abstract Classes vs. Interfaces

Both let you define a type that other classes conform to, but they serve
different purposes:

| | `abstract class` | `interface` |
|---|---|---|
| Fields | can have instance state | only constants (implicitly `public static final`) |
| Constructors | yes | no |
| Multiple inheritance | a class extends only one | a class can implement many |
| Method bodies | mix of abstract + concrete | default methods (since Java 8) + abstract |

```java
interface Shape {
    double area();

    default String describe() {           // default method: has a body
        return "Shape with area " + area();
    }
}

class Circle implements Shape {
    double radius;
    Circle(double radius) { this.radius = radius; }
    @Override
    public double area() { return Math.PI * radius * radius; }
}

Circle c = new Circle(2);
System.out.println(c.describe()); // uses the interface's default implementation
```

Use an abstract class when subclasses share real implementation and state;
use an interface to describe a capability that unrelated classes can all
opt into.

## Records: Immutable Data Carriers

Since Java 16, `record` generates a full immutable data class — fields,
constructor, accessors, `equals()`, `hashCode()`, and `toString()` — all
from a single declaration:

```java
record Point(int x, int y) {}

Point p1 = new Point(1, 2);
Point p2 = new Point(1, 2);
System.out.println(p1.x());             // 1 — accessor, not getX()
System.out.println(p1.equals(p2));       // true — content-based equality, generated for you
System.out.println(p1);                  // Point[x=1, y=2] — generated toString()
```

Records can implement interfaces and add extra methods, but their fields
are always `final` and cannot gain extra mutable state — they are
purpose-built for immutable value types like `Point`, `Range`, or a DTO
returned from an API layer.

## Constructor Chaining and `super`/`this`

`super(...)` calls the parent class's constructor and must be the first
statement if used; `this(...)` calls another constructor in the *same*
class, useful for reducing duplication:

```java
class Vehicle {
    String make;
    Vehicle(String make) { this.make = make; }
}

class Car extends Vehicle {
    int doors;
    Car(String make) {
        this(make, 4); // delegates to the other Car constructor
    }
    Car(String make, int doors) {
        super(make);   // must be first statement — initializes Vehicle's state
        this.doors = doors;
    }
}
```

## Method Overriding Rules

An `@Override` method must have the same signature (or a covariant return
type) and cannot reduce the visibility of the overridden method (e.g., you
cannot override a `public` method with a `protected` one). `@Override` is
optional but strongly recommended — it makes the compiler catch typos in
the method signature that would otherwise silently create an unrelated
overload instead of an override.

## Idiom: Favor Composition Over Deep Inheritance Hierarchies

Deep inheritance chains (`class D extends C extends B extends A`) become
fragile and hard to reason about. Many experienced Java developers prefer
**composition** — holding a reference to another object and delegating to
it — especially when the relationship is "has-a" rather than truly "is-a":

```java
// Composition: Car "has-a" Engine, rather than a fragile inheritance chain
class Engine {
    void start() { System.out.println("Engine starting..."); }
}

class Car {
    private final Engine engine = new Engine(); // composition
    void start() {
        engine.start();
        System.out.println("Car ready to drive");
    }
}
```

## Idiom: Program to an Interface Type

Just as with collections, declare fields and parameters using the
interface/abstract type when possible, so implementations can be swapped:

```java
interface PaymentMethod { void pay(double amount); }
class CreditCard implements PaymentMethod {
    public void pay(double amount) { System.out.println("Paid $" + amount + " by card"); }
}

class Checkout {
    private final PaymentMethod method; // depends on the interface, not a concrete class
    Checkout(PaymentMethod method) { this.method = method; }
    void complete(double amount) { method.pay(amount); }
}
```

## Idiom: Use Records for Simple Immutable Data

Any time a class exists purely to bundle a few related, immutable values
(a coordinate, a config snapshot, a query result row), reach for a
`record` instead of hand-writing a class with a constructor, getters,
`equals`, `hashCode`, and `toString`.

## Common Pitfall: Overloading When You Meant to Override

```java
class Base {
    void greet(Object o) { System.out.println("Base.greet(Object)"); }
}
class Derived extends Base {
    void greet(String s) { System.out.println("Derived.greet(String)"); } // overload, NOT override!
}

Base b = new Derived();
b.greet("hi"); // prints "Base.greet(Object)" — surprising if you expected an override
```

Because the parameter type differs (`Object` vs. `String`), this is a new
*overload*, not an override — and overload resolution happens at compile
time based on the *reference* type (`Base`), not the runtime object.
Always add `@Override` when you intend to override; the compiler will
error out if the signature doesn't actually match a parent method.

## Common Pitfall: Forgetting That `private` Fields Aren't Inherited-Accessible

A subclass cannot directly access a superclass's `private` fields — it
must go through `protected`/`public` accessors, which is precisely
encapsulation working as intended, but surprises newcomers expecting
inheritance to grant full access to everything.

## Worked Example: A Small Shape Hierarchy

This combines interfaces, an abstract class, records, and polymorphism into
one small shape-area calculator.

```java
import java.util.List;

interface Shape {
    double area();
    default String summary() {
        return getClass().getSimpleName() + " has area " + String.format("%.2f", area());
    }
}

abstract class AbstractShape implements Shape {
    protected String color;
    AbstractShape(String color) { this.color = color; }
    String getColor() { return color; }
}

class Circle extends AbstractShape {
    private final double radius;
    Circle(String color, double radius) {
        super(color);
        this.radius = radius;
    }
    @Override
    public double area() { return Math.PI * radius * radius; }
}

class Rectangle extends AbstractShape {
    private final double width, height;
    Rectangle(String color, double width, double height) {
        super(color);
        this.width = width;
        this.height = height;
    }
    @Override
    public double area() { return width * height; }
}

// A record for an immutable measurement result
record AreaReport(String shapeName, double area) {}

public class ShapeDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Shape> shapes = List.of(
                new Circle("red", 3),
                new Rectangle("blue", 4, 5)
        );

        double totalArea = 0;
        for (Shape s : shapes) {
            System.out.println(s.summary()); // polymorphic dispatch to each area()
            totalArea += s.area();
        }

        AreaReport report = new AreaReport("combined", totalArea);
        System.out.println(report); // uses the record's generated toString()
    }
}
```

Each `Shape` implementation overrides `area()` differently, but calling
`.summary()` (a default interface method) or `.area()` through the `Shape`
interface type dispatches polymorphically to the correct subclass at
runtime. `AreaReport` shows a `record` used as a lightweight, immutable
result type.

## Recap

- `extends` gives single-class inheritance; `implements` fulfills one or
  more interface contracts; `@Override` marks polymorphic overrides.
- Encapsulation (`private`/`protected`/`public`) hides state behind a
  controlled API, typically getters/setters.
- Abstract classes share real state and partial implementation across
  subclasses; interfaces describe a capability, optionally with default
  methods, and support multiple implementation.
- `record` generates an entire immutable data class — fields, constructor,
  accessors, equals/hashCode/toString — from one declaration.
- Favor composition over deep inheritance chains, and program against
  interface types rather than concrete classes where practical.

