## Worked Example: A Small User Directory With Optional Lookups

This combines aliasing awareness, `Optional`, and a bounded-size structure
to avoid unbounded growth.

```java
import java.util.*;

public class UserDirectory {
    private final Map<Integer, String> emailsById = new HashMap<>();
    private static final int MAX_ENTRIES = 1000;

    void register(int id, String email) {
        if (emailsById.size() >= MAX_ENTRIES) {
            throw new IllegalStateException("Directory full — enforce a bound to avoid unbounded growth");
        }
        emailsById.put(id, email);
    }

    Optional<String> findEmail(int id) {
        return Optional.ofNullable(emailsById.get(id)); // wraps a possibly-null Map result
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        UserDirectory directory = new UserDirectory();
        directory.register(1, "ada@example.com");

        String email = directory.findEmail(1)
                .map(String::toLowerCase)
                .orElse("unknown@example.com");
        System.out.println(email);

        String missing = directory.findEmail(99)
                .orElseGet(() -> "no-user-found@example.com");
        System.out.println(missing);

        // Demonstrating aliasing vs. an independent copy:
        Map<Integer, String> aliasedView = directory.emailsById; // NOT accessible outside the class in real code,
                                                                    // shown here only to illustrate aliasing
        Map<Integer, String> independentCopy = new HashMap<>(directory.emailsById);
    }
}
```

`Optional.ofNullable` is the idiomatic bridge between a legacy API that
might return `null` (like `Map.get`) and `Optional`-based code.
`findEmail` chains `.map`/`.orElse`/`.orElseGet` rather than manually
checking `isPresent()`, and `MAX_ENTRIES` guards against the unbounded
static-growth memory leak pattern.

## Recap

- The stack holds local variables/parameters per method call; the heap
  holds every object, reclaimed automatically by the garbage collector once
  unreachable.
- Reference variables store a pointer to a heap object, not the object's
  bytes — assigning one reference to another aliases the same object.
- Modern JVM garbage collectors use generational collection (young/old
  generations) and reachability analysis from GC roots, correctly handling
  reference cycles.
- `Optional<T>` makes a possibly-absent return value explicit, and should
  be used with `map`/`orElse`/`orElseThrow` chains rather than a careless
  `.get()`.
- Garbage collection prevents dangling pointers but not logical memory
  leaks — unbounded static collections are the most common real-world Java
  leak pattern.

codeExamples.code:
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

List<Integer> a = new ArrayList<>(List.of(1, 2, 3));
List<Integer> b = a;      // alias, same object
b.add(4);
System.out.println(a);    // [1, 2, 3, 4] -- a changed too

import java.util.Optional;

public class Main {
    static Optional<String> findUserEmail(int id) {
        if (id == 1) return Optional.of("ada@example.com");
        return Optional.empty();
    }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String result = findUserEmail(2).orElse("no-email@example.com");
        System.out.println(result);
    }
}

import java.util.*;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Integer> original = new ArrayList<>(List.of(1, 2, 3));
        List<Integer> alias = original;
        List<Integer> trueCopy = new ArrayList<>(original);

        alias.add(99);
        System.out.println(original); // [1, 2, 3, 99] -- alias mutated it
        System.out.println(trueCopy); // [1, 2, 3]     -- unaffected, independent copy
    }
}

