## Modern File Access With `java.nio.file`

Modern Java code reads and writes files through `java.nio.file.Files` and
`Path`, which are simpler and more capable than the legacy `java.io.File`:

```java
import java.nio.file.*;
import java.util.List;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        Path path = Path.of("data.txt");

        Files.write(path, List.of("hello", "world"));  // writes each string as a line

        List<String> lines = Files.readAllLines(path); // reads the whole file into memory
        for (String line : lines) {
            System.out.println(line);
        }

        String wholeFile = Files.readString(path);      // reads the entire file as one String
        System.out.println(wholeFile.length());
    }
}
```

`Files.readAllLines`/`Files.readString` are convenient for small-to-medium
files; both load the whole content into memory at once.

## try-with-resources for Streams and Writers

For writing incrementally, or reading large files line-by-line rather than
all at once, wrap a `BufferedWriter`/`BufferedReader` in try-with-resources
so it's always closed:

```java
import java.nio.file.*;
import java.io.*;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws IOException {
        Path path = Path.of("log.txt");

        try (BufferedWriter writer = Files.newBufferedWriter(path)) {
            writer.write("first line");
            writer.newLine();
            writer.write("second line");
        } // writer.close() called automatically here

        try (BufferedReader reader = Files.newBufferedReader(path)) {
            String line;
            while ((line = reader.readLine()) != null) {
                System.out.println(line);
            }
        } // reader.close() called automatically here
    }
}
```

## The Standard Library at a Glance

Beyond `java.util` (collections, generics) and `java.io`/`java.nio` (I/O),
a few packages come up constantly:

- **`java.time`** — the modern date/time API (`LocalDate`, `LocalDateTime`,
  `Duration`, `Period`) — always prefer it over the legacy, mutable
  `java.util.Date`.
- **`java.util.stream`** — functional-style pipelines over collections
  (covered in depth in the Collections module).
- **`java.util.regex`** — `Pattern`/`Matcher` for regular expressions.

```java
import java.time.LocalDate;

LocalDate today = LocalDate.of(2026, 7, 28);
LocalDate nextWeek = today.plusDays(7);
System.out.println(nextWeek);          // 2026-08-04
System.out.println(today.isBefore(nextWeek)); // true
```

## Buffered vs. Unbuffered I/O

Raw `FileReader`/`FileWriter` (and their `InputStream`/`OutputStream`
cousins) perform one system call *per read/write operation*, which is slow
for anything beyond trivial files. **Buffered** wrappers
(`BufferedReader`/`BufferedWriter`) batch many small operations into fewer,
larger system calls internally:

```java
import java.io.*;

// Unbuffered: reads one char at a time from disk — slow for large files
try (FileReader raw = new FileReader("big.txt")) {
    int c;
    while ((c = raw.read()) != -1) { /* ... */ }
}

// Buffered: internally batches reads — much faster in practice
try (BufferedReader buffered = new BufferedReader(new FileReader("big.txt"))) {
    String line;
    while ((line = buffered.readLine()) != null) { /* ... */ }
}
```

Always wrap raw streams/readers/writers in a buffered variant unless you
have a specific reason not to.

## try-with-resources Closes in Reverse Order, and Suppresses Exceptions Correctly

When multiple resources are declared in one try-with-resources statement,
they close in the reverse of their declaration order. If the `try` block
throws *and* `close()` also throws, the `close()` exception is
**suppressed** and attached to the original exception (retrievable via
`getSuppressed()`) rather than replacing it — you never silently lose the
original failure:

```java
class NoisyResource implements AutoCloseable {
    public void close() { throw new RuntimeException("close failed"); }
}

try (NoisyResource r = new NoisyResource()) {
    throw new IllegalStateException("main failure");
} catch (IllegalStateException e) {
    System.out.println("Primary: " + e.getMessage());              // "main failure"
    for (Throwable suppressed : e.getSuppressed()) {
        System.out.println("Suppressed: " + suppressed.getMessage()); // "close failed"
    }
}
```

## Streams Over File Lines

`Files.lines(Path)` returns a lazily-read `Stream<String>` directly, which
composes naturally with the Stream API — but because it holds an open file
handle, it **must** be used inside try-with-resources so the file is
closed once the stream is done:

```java
import java.nio.file.*;
import java.util.stream.*;

try (Stream<String> lines = Files.lines(Path.of("data.txt"))) {
    long nonEmptyCount = lines.filter(line -> !line.isBlank()).count();
    System.out.println(nonEmptyCount);
} // the underlying file handle is closed here
```

## Regex With `Pattern`/`Matcher`

For matching more complex patterns than `String.contains`/`startsWith` can
express, `java.util.regex` provides full regular expression support:

```java
import java.util.regex.*;

Pattern emailPattern = Pattern.compile("^[\\w.+-]+@[\\w-]+\\.[a-zA-Z]{2,}$");
Matcher matcher = emailPattern.matcher("ada@example.com");
System.out.println(matcher.matches()); // true
```

`String` itself exposes regex-powered shortcuts too: `.matches(regex)`,
`.replaceAll(regex, replacement)`, and `.split(regex)`.

## Idiom: Always try-with-resources for I/O, Never Manual `close()`

Manually calling `.close()` in a `finally` block is verbose and easy to get
wrong (e.g., forgetting a `null` check, or the `close()` call itself
throwing and hiding the original exception). try-with-resources handles all
of this correctly and should be the default for anything `AutoCloseable`:

```java
// Avoid — verbose and error-prone
BufferedReader reader = null;
try {
    reader = Files.newBufferedReader(Path.of("data.txt"));
    System.out.println(reader.readLine());
} finally {
    if (reader != null) {
        try { reader.close(); } catch (IOException ignored) {}
    }
}

// Prefer
try (BufferedReader reader = Files.newBufferedReader(Path.of("data.txt"))) {
    System.out.println(reader.readLine());
}
```

## Idiom: Prefer `java.time` Over `java.util.Date`/`Calendar`

Legacy `java.util.Date`/`Calendar` are mutable, poorly designed, and easy
to misuse (months are zero-indexed in `Calendar`, a classic bug source).
Modern code should always reach for `java.time` types instead, which are
immutable and thread-safe:

```java
import java.time.LocalDate;
import java.time.Period;

LocalDate birthDate = LocalDate.of(1990, 5, 15);
LocalDate today = LocalDate.of(2026, 7, 28);
Period age = Period.between(birthDate, today);
System.out.println(age.getYears() + " years old");
```

## Common Pitfall: Forgetting Files.lines() Needs Closing

```java
Stream<String> lines = Files.lines(Path.of("data.txt")); // opens a file handle
lines.forEach(System.out::println);
// lines.close() never called — the file handle leaks!
```

Always pair `Files.lines` with try-with-resources, exactly like any other
`AutoCloseable` I/O resource — it's easy to forget precisely because it
*looks* like an in-memory `Stream` rather than something backed by an open
file descriptor.

## Common Pitfall: Reading Huge Files Entirely Into Memory

`Files.readAllLines`/`Files.readString` load the **entire** file content
into memory at once — fine for small config files, but a serious problem
(or an outright `OutOfMemoryError`) for large files. For anything
potentially large, use `Files.lines()` or a `BufferedReader` loop that
processes one line at a time instead.

## Common Pitfall: Platform-Dependent Path Separators

Hand-building paths with string concatenation (`"folder" + "/" + "file.txt"`)
breaks on Windows, which uses `\\`. Always build paths with `Path.of(...)`
or `Paths.get(...)`, which handle platform differences correctly:

```java
Path path = Path.of("folder", "subfolder", "file.txt"); // platform-correct automatically
```

## Worked Example: A Small Log Analyzer

This combines `Path`/`Files`, try-with-resources, `Files.lines` with the
Stream API, and `java.time`, into a small log file analyzer.

```java
import java.nio.file.*;
import java.time.LocalDate;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.stream.*;

public class LogAnalyzer {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        Path logPath = Path.of("app.log");

        // Write a small sample log file first (so this example is runnable standalone)
        Files.write(logPath, List.of(
                "2026-07-28 INFO Server started",
                "2026-07-28 ERROR Failed to connect to database",
                "2026-07-28 INFO Request handled",
                "2026-07-28 ERROR Timeout waiting for response",
                "2026-07-28 WARN Retrying connection"
        ));

        long errorCount;
        List<String> errorLines;

        try (Stream<String> lines = Files.lines(logPath)) {
            // Stream is consumed once inside the try-with-resources block
            errorLines = lines.filter(line -> line.contains("ERROR"))
                               .collect(Collectors.toList());
        } // file handle closed here automatically

        errorCount = errorLines.size();

        System.out.println("Total ERROR lines: " + errorCount);
        errorLines.forEach(System.out::println);

        LocalDate today = LocalDate.now();
        System.out.println("Report generated on: " + today);

        // Clean up the sample file
        Files.deleteIfExists(logPath);
    }
}
```

`Files.lines` is opened and consumed entirely inside the try-with-resources
block (streams can only be traversed once, and must be fully consumed
before the underlying file handle closes); `Collectors.toList()` materializes
the filtered lines into a plain `List` that can be used safely after the
stream itself has closed; and `LocalDate.now()` demonstrates the modern
date/time API for a timestamped report line.

## Recap

- `java.nio.file.Files`/`Path` are the modern way to read and write files;
  `Files.readAllLines`/`readString` are convenient but load the whole file
  into memory.
- try-with-resources should be the default for any `AutoCloseable` I/O
  resource — it closes correctly (in reverse order) even when exceptions
  occur, and preserves suppressed exceptions rather than hiding them.
- `Files.lines(Path)` returns a lazy `Stream<String>` that must be used
  inside try-with-resources since it holds an open file handle.
- Buffered readers/writers batch I/O operations for real performance gains
  over raw, unbuffered streams.
- Prefer the modern `java.time` API over legacy `Date`/`Calendar`, and
  `Path.of(...)` over hand-built path strings, for correctness across
  platforms.

codeExamples.code:
import java.nio.file.*;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        Path path = Path.of("data.txt");
        Files.write(path, List.of("hello", "world"));

        List<String> lines = Files.readAllLines(path);
        String upper = lines.stream()
                .map(String::toUpperCase)
                .collect(Collectors.joining(", "));
        System.out.println(upper);
    }
}

import java.nio.file.*;
import java.util.stream.*;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        Path path = Path.of("data.txt");
        Files.write(path, List.of("one", "", "two", ""));

        try (Stream<String> lines = Files.lines(path)) {
            long nonEmpty = lines.filter(l -> !l.isBlank()).count();
            System.out.println(nonEmpty);
        }
    }
}

import java.time.LocalDate;
import java.time.Period;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        LocalDate birthDate = LocalDate.of(1990, 5, 15);
        LocalDate today = LocalDate.of(2026, 7, 28);
        Period age = Period.between(birthDate, today);
        System.out.println(age.getYears() + " years old");
    }
}

